# cppcheck MISRA-C:2012 suppressions for libpfc.
# See flight/docs/misra-deviations.md for the full rule-by-rule justification -- every entry here
# is a project-wide policy decision made after reading actual flagged source lines for each rule
# (not a blanket "make CI green"). Each suppression line below must contain ONLY the rule ID (no
# inline trailing text) to be safe against cppcheck's suppression-list parser; all reasoning lives
# in the '#' comment above each one and in misra-deviations.md.

# Confirmed TOOL LIMITATIONS (false positives), not real findings:

# comma-separated declarator lists ("int a, b, c;") misidentified as the comma operator by
# cppcheck's MISRA addon -- confirmed by reading every flagged line in this codebase.
misra-c2012-12.3

# "could be static" on functions declared with external linkage in pfc_internal.h and genuinely
# called from other translation units -- cppcheck analyzes one file at a time here (make misra
# runs it over src/ without whole-program mode) and can't see the cross-file usage.
misra-c2012-8.7

# Deliberate, verified-safe patterns that conflict with an Advisory MISRA rule:

# Single point of exit -- conflicts with the project's deliberate defensive early-return-on-
# invalid-input style used throughout (57 occurrences, by far the largest category).
misra-c2012-15.5

# Explicit widening casts for safe array-index/pointer arithmetic (e.g. (size_t)(y-1u)*width) --
# deliberate overflow prevention, not accidental narrowing/widening.
misra-c2012-10.8

# Operator-precedence parenthesization (style-only, zero safety impact -- precedence is
# well-defined either way in every flagged expression).
misra-c2012-12.1

# void* <-> object-pointer conversions, unavoidable in generic buffer-handling C (encode/decode
# operate on raw byte buffers of varying element types via void* in the public API).
misra-c2012-11.5

# Mixed-width integer arithmetic in bit/byte-level codec math (unavoidable in this domain).
misra-c2012-10.7

# Composite expression assigned to a wider essential type -- same family as 10.7/10.8, deliberate
# widening, not accidental.
misra-c2012-10.6

# uint8_t stream bytes compared against char literals ('P','F','C','1' magic bytes) -- standard,
# unavoidable when checking buffer bytes against ASCII constants.
misra-c2012-10.4

# Deliberate narrowing casts (uint32_t -> uint8_t byte truncation), already verified more
# precisely by -Wconversion -Werror, which passes cleanly across the whole codebase.
misra-c2012-10.3

# `n >> 31` on signed int32_t in the zigzag encode (pfc_model.c pfc_zigzag) -- relies on
# arithmetic right-shift of a negative value, implementation-defined pre-C23 but universal on
# every real twos-complement target, INCLUDING all three of this project's actual cross-compile
# targets (PowerPC/RAD750, SPARC/LEON, RISC-V) -- verified, not assumed; see misra-deviations.md.
misra-c2012-10.1

# Deliberate side effect in a && operand: the Subbotin range-coder renormalisation idiom
# (pfc_arith.c) -- verified single-evaluation, no double-side-effect risk; a well-known pattern
# from range-coder literature, not an accident.
misra-c2012-13.5

# Function parameters reused as scratch/working variables (e.g. pfc_bitlen's `u >>= 1` loop) --
# safe by construction, C parameters are pass-by-value, no caller-visible aliasing.
misra-c2012-17.8

# Integer used directly as a boolean controlling expression (`if (x)` on a flag/count), extremely
# common and unambiguous in context.
misra-c2012-14.4

# PFC_WORKMEM_BYTES (include/pfc.h) looks unused because it's PUBLIC API for external callers,
# not used internally by the library itself -- intentional, not dead code.
misra-c2012-2.5

# --enable=warning,style also turns on cppcheck's own (non-MISRA) general checks, which surfaced
# two more findings once the misra-c2012-* list above was applied and stopped masking them:

# Exact duplicate of the misra-c2012-10.1 finding already justified above -- pfc_model.c's
# pfc_zigzag, `n >> 31` on signed int32_t. Same code, same reasoning, different cppcheck error ID
# (cppcheck's generic portability check vs. the MISRA addon's rule numbering for the same issue).
shiftTooManyBitsSigned

# pfc_columnar.c: a `uint32_t c;` declared at the top of the per-block loop (alongside every other
# local used anywhere in that loop body) is only actually used inside one nested branch. cppcheck
# suggests narrowing its scope; doing so would mean declaring it partway through the block, which
# is exactly the declaration-after-statement pattern this codebase deliberately avoids (corrected
# twice in this same session to keep all locals declared at the top of the block they're in) --
# kept as-is for consistency with that convention rather than narrowed for this one variable.
variableScope

